# Measure which layer misplaces the bottom-row target effect.
#
# Build with debug info first, or none of this resolves:
#
#   make -C theos RBPDBG=1 RBPDBGINFO=1
#
# Then, with the play screen up:
#
#   (lldb) command source /var/jb/var/mobile/rb458-targets.lldb
#   (lldb) continue
#
# The top row of targets aligns and the bottom row does not, so two sprites that should coincide
# have different Y. Reading layer after layer against the disassembly found seven mechanisms that
# were all faithful, which is a sign the wrong question was being asked: the useful question is not
# "is this layer correct" but "which layer emits the sprite that is in the wrong place".
#
# Every sprite goes through SetSpritePosition or SetSpritePositionXY, so breaking there and printing
# the owning batch with the position names the culprit directly. `bt 3` gives the emitting layer,
# and `this` distinguishes the batches, so the pair that should coincide is visible as two lines
# with the same intent and different Y.
#
# Breakpoint commands run without stopping (auto-continue), so the game keeps running and the log
# accumulates. Expect a lot of output: let it run a second or two, then interrupt and read back.

breakpoint set --name "ne::C_SPRITE_INSTANCING_2D::SetSpritePositionXY"
breakpoint command add --script-type none
frame variable this nIndex x y
bt 3
continue
DONE

breakpoint set --name "ne::C_SPRITE_INSTANCING_2D::SetSpritePosition"
breakpoint command add --script-type none
frame variable this nIndex position
bt 3
continue
DONE

# If the two breakpoints produce too much to read, disable one and re-run. If neither resolves,
# check `image lookup -t neGLESRenderer` first: no output there means the build carries symbols but
# no DWARF, and RBPDBGINFO was not set.
